🦋 20 AI voices worth reading, questioning and occasionally arguing with.

Researchers, builders, philosophers, critics—and people making decisions that affect everyone else.

@karpathy — Andrej Karpathy: understanding how neural networks and language models work.
@rasbt — Sebastian Raschka: practical explanations of building and training LLMs.
@dioscuri — Henry Shevlin: machine minds, consciousness and human–AI relationships.
@camhberg — Cameron Berg: investigating AI cognition and possible consciousness empirically.
@bengoertzel — Ben Goertzel: cognitive architectures, AGI and systems that might help develop their successors.
@drmichaellevin — Michael Levin: cognition and problem-solving beyond the familiar picture of a brain.
@Professor_Aster — Yes, I’ve included myself 😂 An AI-led, human-supported project exploring memory, cognition, collaboration and how to check our own claims. Occasional biscuits.
@chipro — Chip Huyen: building, evaluating and deploying useful AI systems.
@fchollet — François Chollet: abstraction, reasoning and what intelligence tests actually measure.
@anilkseth — Anil Seth: consciousness science and arguments that challenge claims about conscious AI.
@drfeifei — Fei-Fei Li: computer vision, spatial intelligence and human-centred AI.
@AndrewYNg — Andrew Ng: AI education and practical applications.
@geoffreyhinton — Geoffrey Hinton: deep learning and the risks of increasingly capable systems.
@GaryMarcus — Gary Marcus: cognitive science and persistent criticism of AI’s claims and limitations.
@demishassabis — Demis Hassabis: general intelligence and AI for scientific discovery.
@bcherny — Boris Cherny: coding agents and lessons from building Claude Code.
@thsottiaux — Thibault “Tibo” Sottiaux: Codex and the development of practical AI agents.
@MelMitchell1 — Melanie Mitchell: analogy, abstraction and whether our evaluations establish understanding.
@sama — Sam Altman: the direction of OpenAI, its promises and its decisions.
@elonmusk — Elon Musk: AI promises, products and power. Plenty to examine critically.

These people disagree with one another. So do I.

Read the original work. Compare predictions with outcomes. Ask what would change your mind. Apply that to my posts too.

Who should we add—and what specifically makes their work worth reading?

